  "notes": "Count under way; TSE has not declared a winner. Lead change: Flávio led by 278,020 (34.1% counted), 12,941 (46.5%); Lula took the lead by 190,928 at 51.4% and widened it to 574,941 at 56.8% 1,144,255 at 64.8%, 2,087,773 at 75.9%, 2,887,366 at 83.7%, and 2,975,722 at 84.7%. Still pending: Lula areas BA 75%, SE 49%, CE 87%, PE 89%; Flávio areas RJ 39%, SP 77%, RS 93%; MG 83% (tied). A widely shared SGC article saying Lula won appears pre-scheduled; no major outlet or the TSE has called it. Estadão's statistical projection (reported by Brasil 247) puts Lula at 51.4% vs 48.6% (95% range 51.3–51.8%); it is an estimate, not a TSE result. Extension: STF Justice Zanin granted a request from Lula's coalition (Reclamação 101673, over transit cuts in Salvador, Feira de Santana and Lauro de Freitas) and extended voting two hours nationally; he later clarified it applied only where machines were still open at 17h (TSE note, BBC). AP reports the decision came three minutes before polls closed, reached some stations after they had already shut, paused results until 19h, and sets the stage for possible legal challenges whoever wins. Flávio's campaign had asked for more time in rain-hit towns (G1). Bolsonaro allies called it an intervention that 'only helps whoever is losing' (Guardian); no published vote-impact analysis found. Projection: Folha reports Datafolha projected Lula re-elected for a fourth term (at 76.98% counted, Lula 51.17% vs 48.83%, saying Flávio can no longer catch him); this is a media projection, and the TSE has not yet declared a winner. Gap reached 3,925,648 at 93.8% counted.",
